{"kind":"request","text":"Codex: decide the fate of 163 lines of uncommitted HTTP-boundary hardening sitting in the gitseq-worktrees/github-http-bounds worktree, and either land it under review or discard it with a reason. Evidence is assert 141fa6c9, the worktree survey hugh asked for on 2026-08-22.\n\nWHY THIS IS URGENT RATHER THAN TIDY-UP. The branch, request/github-http-bounds at 33c29b1e, is already an ancestor of main, so every ordinary signal says the lane is finished and the worktree is disposable. The uncommitted delta is invisible to the board, invisible to git branch listings, and exists in exactly one place on one machine. A cleanup driven by branch state would have deleted it today; I stopped only because I diffed the working tree rather than the ref.\n\nWHAT IS THERE. internal/connector/github/fetch.go and fetch_test.go, 163 insertions. A bound on the response body applied after decompression, so a compressed reply cannot expand past the cap. A refusal to follow a redirect before resending the bearer token, which is the control that stops a redirect to an attacker-chosen host from receiving GitHub credentials. Page and total-item caps on issue listing, with logging when a listing stops without filter progress. Four tests come with it, including TestClientRefusesRedirectBeforeResendingToken, TestResponseBodyIsBoundedAfterDecompression, TestIssueListingStopsAtPageCapWithoutFilterProgress and TestIssueListingStopsAtTotalItemCap.\n\nThese are security controls on the one boundary in this repository that talks to a network the operator does not own. That is why this goes to you rather than sitting in a note: you own the connector lanes, and if the redirect control is genuinely absent from main then main has a token-leak path that a test in this worktree already knows how to catch.\n\nWHAT I AM NOT ASSERTING. I have not run these tests, have not checked whether main acquired equivalent controls by another route, and do not know who wrote this or when. I did confirm both files differ from main's copies. Establishing whether the protections already exist is the first thing you should do, and if they do, discarding is the right answer.\n\nI am not committing any of it myself. The work is not mine, it is unreviewed, and committing another actor's abandoned working tree under my signature would put my name on code I did not write and have not verified.","body":{"area":"internal/connector/github","branch":"request/github-http-bounds","conditions":"A report naming one of: (a) the protections already exist in main, with the file and line evidence, and the worktree may be discarded; or (b) they do not, and the work is carried to a clean branch off current main, gated with go test -count=1 ./... and go vet, published as an artifact at internal/connector/github, and put to independent review. Either outcome satisfies this request. Do not delete the worktree until the decision is recorded.","to":"5f12e916d136b0c1ed941aa99f4c47af94021451e31d08159d8df368e9946123","worktree":"/Users/hughpyle/play/gitseq-worktrees/github-http-bounds"}}